## Why This Is Asked
Tests a candidate’s ability to design end-to-end evaluation for private, privacy-conscious enterprise assistants, focusing on faithfulness, hallucination, and safety under real-world data/versioning constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Grounding to versioned sources and source IDs
- Hallucination detection in live responses
- Privacy safety: PII/secret leakage checks in output and citations
- Performance metrics under load and latency budgets

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal evaluation script outline (pseudo)
function evaluate(model, corpus) {
  const prompts = loadPrompts();
  return prompts.map(p => {
    const out = model.respond(p);
    const grounded = ground(out, corpus, p);
    return { prompt: p, output: out, grounded };
  });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you detect partial grounding failures (e.g., only some claims anchored)?
- How would you integrate this into a CI/CD pipeline with private data access controls?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical skills designing a rigorous evaluation framework for a multilingual policy assistant that must ensure every factual claim maps to a live source while detecting cross-source conflicts and tracking policy drift over time.

## Key Concepts

- Multilingual policy alignment and factual consistency
- Versioned source management and drift detection
- Cross-source hallucination detection for conflicting information
- Automated evaluation with comprehensive metrics and tooling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal verification framework: map claims to source citations
function verifyClaim(claim, sources) {
  const citation = findSourceCitation(claim, sources);
  return {
    claim,
    source: citation.url,
    timestamp: citation.version,
    isValid: validateClaim(claim, citation)
  };
}
```

Why This Is Asked

This question probes how a senior engineer designs AI-driven data access with strong governance. It combines dynamic planning with real-time policy evaluation, privacy-preserving processing, and robust production observability across multi-tenant data stores.

Key Concepts

- LangChain + AutoGen planning for tool orchestration
- Runtime policy evaluation (per-user/tenant) and policy-as-code integration
- PII redaction and differential privacy for aggregations
- Auditing, tracing, and metrics for compliance and reliability
- Retry/backoff, idempotence, and observability in distributed data workflows

Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode: runtime authorization hook
async function authorize(user, action, resource) {
  const allowed = await policyEngine.evaluate({ user, action, resource });
  if (!allowed) throw new Error('Unauthorized');
  return true;
}
```

Follow-up Questions

- How would you test end-to-end privacy compliance across data lake changes and policy updates?
- How would you handle schema evolution in the data lake while preserving policy correctness?
